# Copy to app/signing.local (untracked) to sign with your own Apple Development certificate.
#
# Without this file the app builds ad-hoc, which is fine for running it from the build folder. It is worth
# setting up if you install the app: macOS keys the permissions it grants — Documents access, notifications —
# to the signature, and an ad-hoc signature is a fresh identity on every build, so every rebuild asks again.
#
# You need a certificate, which signing in to Xcode does not create on its own:
#   Xcode → Settings → Accounts → your Apple ID → Manage Certificates… → + → Apple Development
# Then read the identity and the team (the team is the OU in the certificate's subject):
#   security find-identity -v -p codesigning
#
# If that prints "0 valid identities found" while the certificate is clearly there, the chain is broken rather
# than the certificate: `security find-identity -p codesigning` without -v lists identities that exist but do
# not validate. Usually the Apple WWDR intermediate is missing or expired — install the current one from
# https://www.apple.com/certificateauthority/

COUNCIL_CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY="Apple Development"
COUNCIL_DEVELOPMENT_TEAM="XXXXXXXXXX"
